A||||10||jlhuber||0-60 on modified '88GT 3800SII?||||5||
Z||000000||jlhuber||02-26-2001||11:53 AM||||Do you guys think that 4.3 - 4.6 sec 0-60s would be possible on an '88GT 5spd, sunroof, 3800SII pushing ~270bhp, trunk cut out, no A/C, no spare tire, battery relocate, no sub, with a pilot that knows how to shift?  I am getting another Fiero the next time I go home to Michigan.  There is a guy in my home town who collects them and says he would sell me one since he bought mine.  He has a couple of '88GTs for me to chose from, but I would be doing the above mods to it.  I am trying to beat a stock GMC Syclone (0-60@ 4.6 from the factory).  Think this is possible with the above setup?  <P>------------------<BR>--Jess--<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/blackeye2/double0smile.gif">   <BR>If you expect the unexpected, was it really even unexpected in the first place?||||reg||5
Z||000001||maninblack||02-26-2001||09:29 PM||||is there a reason why you only pushing the series 2 to only 270 hp ?||||reg||1
Z||000002||jlhuber||02-26-2001||10:58 PM||||I don't need a monstrous motor, unless it is required to get to my performance goals.  I am aiming for a 4.3-4.6 0-60 and still get 20 or more mpg, if it is possible to have both.  I will take acceleration/speed over economy if I have to chose.  Anywho, I figure 240bhp from the factory (motor is from a '99 GTP with 11K miles) with a few minor mods (chip, s/c pulley, igniton, and a few others) should put it up to ~270bhp.  From what I understand, the stock 240bhp will push a Fiero pretty good.  If lighten the Fiero a bit and increase the hp, I should be able to end up where I am trying for.  I was just wondering if you guys think that will be enough hp for what I want, or should I try to push some more hp from the motor?||||reg||1
Z||000003||pontiacs4me||02-27-2001||01:32 AM||||Horsepower makes the car go fast on the top end, torque makes the car go fast from 0-60.  You keep talking about raising the hp, the more you do the more you raise the rpm range, but you lower the effective torque range.  Concentrate on port velocity.  What size of pulley are you running?  A friend of mine has a 3800 sc in a 91 lumina, running a 3.4 inch pulley.  His car runs in the mid 13's and gets 26 mpg.  Ive heard from a few sources that fieros running the stock 240 horse L67 should be in the low 13's with 30+ mpg.  A 3.4 inch pulley and some 92 octane with an ignition upgrade and cold air induction should put you in the mid 12s and gas mileage wont drop off much at all.  You should be able to get at least 25.  I believe mid 12s in the quarter is about 4.5 0-60.  <BR>Just concentrate on port velocity, not so much the high rpm hp, get some cold supercharged air ramming in there and you will get your goal no problem.||||reg||1
Z||000004||jlhuber||02-27-2001||11:11 AM||||I realize that I need torque to get me up & going and hp for top end speed.  Everyone views hp/torque in different ways and I said hp in my post with the thought that it would be easier to discuss it like that.  Though now that I think about it, your right, I should have referenced torque instead.  My bad.  The motor going into my next Fiero is completely stock as it sits, haven't decided on which size/brand parts that I am going to put on it, I only have planned what I want changed.  Gotta wait till I am back in Michigan before I can do anything anyways.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Will||02-27-2001||01:48 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by jlhuber:<BR><B>I realize that I need torque to get me up & going and hp for top end speed.  Everyone views hp/torque in different ways and I said hp in my post with the thought that it would be easier to discuss it like that.  Though now that I think about it, your right, I should have referenced torque instead.  My bad.  The motor going into my next Fiero is completely stock as it sits, haven't decided on which size/brand parts that I am going to put on it, I only have planned what I want changed.  Gotta wait till I am back in Michigan before I can do anything anyways.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The mods you listed earlier will not change the shape of your power curve. The smaller pulley will spin the blower faster at all RPM's and increase torque the same amount across your powerband. The RPM's for peak power and torque will not change significantly.<BR>To get 0-60 times that low you need serious traction and a hard launch. I don't doubt that much power and torque could get your car to accelerate like that, but you'd need a chassis setup to match the engine, and if you use a manual, you should be worried about transmission life.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Wannabe||02-27-2001||02:27 PM||||The Syclone has a serious advantage from 0-60 because of four/all wheel drive traction. It is shaped like a brick though, so with a similar or better power to weigt ratio, the Fiero will be much beter above 60. If you read some of the orignal tests for the Syclone, the testers commented on how dramatically the accelleration drops off at the top end. I don't think the Syclones ever got into the 12's stock.||||reg||1
Z||000007||THE BEAST||02-27-2001||04:14 PM||||"Syclones". What is that? I'm lost here (is that a new concept car?)<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000008||Greg Piet||02-27-2001||04:31 PM||||Cyclone = Vette killer with truck body made by GMC<BR>4.3liter twin turbo if I am not mistaken<P>------------------<BR>SCCA Region 72<BR>#47 CSP<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.physics.unlv.edu/~gregpiet/Jan1617s.jpg">  <BR>Our lady of blessed acceleration dont fail me now!||||reg||1
Z||000009||jlhuber||02-27-2001||04:53 PM||||&gt;So what's a Syclone anyway? <P>The GMC Syclone is a turbocharged, AWD pickup truck that has the distinction of being the quickest truck ever produced. They were manufactured by Production Automotive Services, Inc (PAS), who also did the '89 Turbo Trans Am. <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1359159&a=10128381&p=39139907">  <P>&gt;Oh, yeah, that's that nasty little truck with the Buick GN motor. <P>Not quite. The Syclone has a 4.3L Vortec (Same motor as the Chevy) V-6 that pumps out 280 HP in stock form. The Syclone has a liquid- cooled intercooler, whereas the Buick's is air cooled. The Buick also has Sequential fuel injection, the Syclone has multi-port. Although unconfirmed, there has been talk on the mailing list that the actual horsepower rating of the Syclone engine is 330; however due to the 280 HP rating of the transmission, the lesser figure was used in promotions. But, as mentioned, no data or evidence can support this theory. <P>&gt;Ok, so what's a Typhoon then? <P>The Typhoon is essentialy the same chassis/powertrain, but with the "Jimmy" (Blazer) bodystyle. It's also a little more plush than a Sy, with leather seats, power mirrors, and nicer interior trimmings, such as a CD player. They also had a rear air-ride suspension to take some of the harshness out of the road bumps. The Typhoon seats five, whereas the Sy will only seat two (but the Sy has more cargo space!). <P>&gt;There weren't very many of them made, were there? <P>Nope, only 2998 '91 Sys were built, 2500 '92 Tys and 2200 '93 Tys. <P>ENGINE:<BR>Type:             V6<BR>Disp.:            262 C.I. (4.3L) w/single turbo<BR>Horsepower:       280 BHP @ 4400 (Syclone) 285 BHP @4400 (Typhoon)<BR>Torque:           350 lb-ft @ 3600 (Syclone) 350 lb-ft @ 3600 (Typhoon)<BR>RPO:              LB4<BR>Bore:             4.00<BR>Stroke:           3.48<BR>Comp. Ratio:      8.35:1<BR>Firing Order:     1-6-5-4-3-2<BR>Oil Pres. (Min)   6 psi@ 1000 RPM, 18 psi@ 2000 RPM, 24 psi@ 4000 RPM<P>Oh yeah, if you need any more info on the Sy/Ty go to  <A HREF="http://www.syty.net" TARGET=_blank>www.syty.net</A>  or  <A HREF="http://www.syty.org" TARGET=_blank>www.syty.org</A> <BR>------------------<BR>--Jess--<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/blackeye2/double0smile.gif">    <BR>If you expect the unexpected, was it really even unexpected in the first place?<p>[This message has been edited by jlhuber (edited 02-27-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000010||jlhuber||02-27-2001||05:00 PM||||No 12s huh?!?  Though you are right, these are obviously not stock. <P>There have been numerous Syclones get into the low 13s.<BR>  <BR>10.6x @ 1xx.xx  The Hoover's, 1991 Syclone 60' - 1.xx KCIR, Kansas City , MO <BR> <BR>10.73 @ 123.29  Dean Ollar, 1993 Typhoon 60' - 1.45 Nat'l Trails Raceway, Columbus, OH <P>10.9x @ 1xx.xx  Guy Lavy, 1991 Syclone 60' - 1.xx KCIR, Kansas City, MO <BR> <BR>11.07 @ 123.70  Tony Gomes, 1991 Syclone <BR>60' - 1.73 Lebanon Raceway, New York <BR> <BR>11.21 @ 120.84  Dennis Schultz, 1991 Syclone <P>11.53 @ 115.64  Jerry McCoy, 1991 Syclone 60' - 1.58  Mountain Parkway Motorplex, Clay City, KY <P>11.62 @ 114.89  Dennis Raab, 1991 Syclone Capitol Raceway, Croften, MD <BR> <BR>11.66 @ 115.82  John Brinson, 1991 Syclone 60' - 1.589<BR>1/8 - 92.83 mph  Killcare Raceway, Xenia, OH <BR> <BR>11.80 @ 117.00  Douglas Derby, 1991 Syclone Lapeer International Dragway <P>11.90 @ 114.20  Mark Murphy, 1992 Typhoon <BR>    <BR>11.95 @ 113.4  Sean Morris, 1992 Typhoon 1/8 - 7.639 @ 90.46 60' - 1.70  Palmdale, CA(3000 ft)  <p>[This message has been edited by jlhuber (edited 02-27-2001).]||||reg||12
